Title: The Fayoum Letter
Credit: Original screenplay
Author: Goldenrod fixtures
Source: Written for this repository. Original work, MIT licensed with the project.
Draft date: 2026-08-01
Notes: WHITE production draft. Scenes locked.

====

INT. CAIRO AIRPORT, ARRIVALS - DAY #1#

RANA MOSTAFA, 34, pulls a single case through the crowd. She has not slept. She stops under the exit sign and does not go through it.

TAREK (V.O.)
(phone)
I can come and get you.

RANA
I know the way.

TAREK (V.O.)
Rana. It's been eleven years.

RANA
I know the way, Tarek.

She hangs up and walks out into the heat.

INT. FLAT, HALLWAY - ZAMALEK - DAY #5#

Dust sheets over everything. RANA stands in the doorway with the case still in her hand. The flat smells of a closed window.

She puts the case down. She does not take her shoes off.

MRS. WADIDA (O.S.)
(through the door)
Rana? Is that Rana?

RANA
It's me, Auntie.

INT. FLAT, MOTHER'S BEDROOM - DAY #8#

RANA pulls a sheet off the dressing table. Underneath: perfume bottles, a hairbrush, a photograph of a woman standing in an olive grove.

RANA picks up the photograph. On the back, in her mother's hand: FAYOUM, 2009.

She sets it face down.

INT. STAIRWELL - DAY #12#

MRS. WADIDA, 70s, blocks the stairs the way only a neighbour can.

MRS. WADIDA
She talked about you. Every week, she talked about you.

RANA
She had Tarek.

MRS. WADIDA
She had the land. That was what she had.
(beat)
Every summer, the two of you under those trees. She never sold a metre of it. Not in forty years.

RANA says nothing.

MRS. WADIDA (CONT'D)
Go and see it. Before you go back.

INT. FLAT, MOTHER'S BEDROOM - NIGHT #18#

RANA has emptied the wardrobe onto the bed. At the bottom, behind the shoes, a TIN BOX.

She opens it. Papers. A key. A LETTER, sealed, her own name on the front in her mother's handwriting.

She sits down on the floor with her back to the bed.

She opens the letter and reads it.

INSERT - THE LETTER

Handwritten, dated MARCH 2019.

"...so I sold the Fayoum land in the spring. All of it. Dr Sami said the treatment abroad was the only thing left to try and I would not take money from either of you. Tarek does not know. Please do not be angry with him for that..."

BACK TO SCENE

RANA reads it twice. She puts her hand over her mouth.

INT. FLAT, KITCHEN - NIGHT #22#

The LETTER is open on the kitchen table. RANA has the phone to her ear. A lawyer's hold music.

YOUSSEF (V.O.)
(phone)
Madam Rana. I am sorry for your loss.

RANA
I need to ask you about the Fayoum land.

YOUSSEF (V.O.)
(phone)
Ah.

RANA
She sold it. Was it you who did the papers?

YOUSSEF (V.O.)
(phone)
It was. She asked me not to file it with the family address.

RANA
And my brother signed nothing.

YOUSSEF (V.O.)
(phone)
Your brother was not told, madam. That was her instruction.

RANA puts the phone down and looks at the letter for a long time.

INT. FLAT, LIVING ROOM - NIGHT #24#

TAREK MOSTAFA, 41, still in his work clothes, stands just inside the door. RANA has not offered him a seat.

TAREK
You could have called me from the airport.

RANA
You knew she sold Fayoum.

TAREK
What?

RANA
Eleven years I am not here and you let her sell the land and neither of you says one word to me.

TAREK
Rana, the land is —

RANA
Every metre of it. While you were forty minutes away.

TAREK
(quietly)
Say that again.

RANA
She sold Fayoum, Tarek.

TAREK sits down without being asked.

EXT. CAFE, ZAMALEK - DAY #26#

RANA and TAREK on the pavement side. Two coffees. Neither touched.

TAREK
Forty people came. The whole building came.

RANA
I know. Auntie Wadida told me.

TAREK
You could have come for the burial. Not the paperwork.

RANA
I came when I could come.

A waiter clears the next table. They wait for him to finish.

TAREK
Will you stay to the forty days?

RANA
I don't know yet.

EXT. STREET, ZAMALEK - NIGHT #27#

RANA walks. The street is doing what it does at nine at night: bread, cats, a wedding two buildings down.

She stops outside a lit window and watches a family eat, and then she keeps walking.

EXT. FAYOUM LAND - DAY #31#

Flat green, then the olive grove, older than the road. RANA stands at the fence with the key in her hand. It opens nothing here any more.

A boy on a bicycle watches her from the track.

RANA
(to herself)
Forty years.

INT. FARMHOUSE - DAY #33#

HAMDY, 60s, pours tea nobody asked for. The room has someone else's furniture in it.

HAMDY
You are the daughter. You have her face.

RANA
When did you buy it?

HAMDY
Seven years back. Spring.
(beat)
She would not go below the asking. Not one pound. She said it was for a doctor.

RANA
She told you that.

HAMDY
She told me that.

EXT. OLIVE GROVE - DUSK #36#

TAREK finds RANA sitting against a trunk. He does not sit.

TAREK
I have read it four times.

RANA
I know.

TAREK
She wrote to you. Not to me.

RANA
She wrote to me because I was the one who was not here.

TAREK sits down against the next tree.

INT. FLAT, MOTHER'S BEDROOM - NIGHT #40#

The wardrobe is empty. The photograph from scene 8 is face up now. RANA puts the letter back in the tin box and the key on top of it.

She closes the lid.

EXT. CAIRO AIRPORT, DEPARTURES - DAY #44#

RANA at the barrier with the same single case. TAREK on the other side of it.

TAREK
Forty days.

RANA
Forty days.

She goes through. He stays until she is out of sight.

FADE OUT.
